/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/palo-alto-ngfw.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("palo-alto-ngfw", [["pa1",0,1,"A team wants to insert a new firewall between an existing router and core switch without changing any IP addresses or routes, while still enforcing App-ID policy. Which interface type fits?",["Virtual wire","Layer 3 with new subnets","Tap","Loopback"],0,"A virtual wire binds two interfaces transparently with no IP or MAC addressing, so it drops into an existing path with no readdressing while still applying security policy. Layer 3 would need new subnets and routes.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — interfaces",[null,"A Layer 3 interface routes between subnets, so inserting it would require new IP addressing and route changes, which the team wants to avoid.","Tap mode only receives a mirrored copy of traffic; it is not inline and cannot enforce App-ID policy on the real flow.","A loopback is a logical, always-up Layer 3 interface used for services like management or GlobalProtect; it cannot sit between two devices in a path."],2],["pa2",0,1,"A security team wants to evaluate App-ID visibility on a SPAN port copy of production traffic without any risk of blocking it. Which interface mode should they use?",["Virtual wire","Layer 2","Tap","HA"],2,"Tap mode passively receives mirrored traffic and can report on it but cannot block. A virtual wire sits inline, so a policy mistake could drop real traffic.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — interfaces",["A virtual wire is inline between two devices, so a policy mistake could drop production traffic, which is exactly the risk to avoid.","Layer 2 interfaces are inline and switch frames between ports, so they also forward (and can block) live traffic rather than passively watching a SPAN copy.",null,"HA is an interface type reserved for high-availability links between peer firewalls; it does not receive or analyze mirrored user traffic."],2],["pa3",0,1,"Which statement about security zones on a PAN-OS firewall is correct?",["A zone can contain only one interface","An interface can belong to only one zone","Zones are optional for Layer 3 interfaces","A Layer 2 interface can join a Layer 3 zone"],1,"Every traffic-carrying interface must be assigned to exactly one zone, and the zone type must match the interface type. A zone can hold many interfaces.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — zones",["A zone can contain many interfaces of the same type; the one-to-one rule applies in the other direction (each interface to one zone).",null,"Zones are mandatory for every traffic-carrying interface, including Layer 3; without a zone the interface cannot pass traffic.","The zone type must match the interface type, so a Layer 2 interface can only join a Layer 2 zone, not a Layer 3 zone."],1],["pa4",0,1,"With no custom rules added, how does a PAN-OS firewall treat traffic between two interfaces in the same zone, and between two different zones?",["Both allowed","Both denied","Intrazone denied, interzone allowed","Intrazone allowed, interzone denied"],3,"The predefined intrazone-default rule allows traffic within a zone and interzone-default denies traffic between zones. Both can be overridden to change logging or action.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — zones",["Interzone traffic is not allowed by default; the interzone-default rule denies it until you add an allow rule.","Intrazone traffic is not denied by default; the intrazone-default rule allows traffic that stays within the same zone.","This reverses the defaults: intrazone-default allows and interzone-default denies.",null],1],["pa5",0,1,"An engineer bundles four 10G ports toward a switch pair running LACP to get more bandwidth and link redundancy. What should be configured on the firewall?",["Four separate Layer 3 subinterfaces","A virtual wire with four members","An aggregate Ethernet interface group","A tunnel interface per port"],2,"An aggregate Ethernet (AE) group combines physical members into one logical interface and can negotiate LACP. Separate subinterfaces would not share load as one link.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — interfaces",["Subinterfaces split one physical port into VLANs; four separate Layer 3 subinterfaces do not bond ports into one LACP bundle for shared bandwidth.","A virtual wire binds exactly two interfaces transparently and cannot aggregate four ports or negotiate LACP.",null,"Tunnel interfaces are logical endpoints for IPsec, GRE or GlobalProtect; they have nothing to do with bundling physical links."],2],["pa6",0,1,"A Layer 3 interface must carry VLANs 10, 20 and 30 from an 802.1Q trunk, each routed and placed in its own zone. What is the standard approach?",["Create tagged Layer 3 subinterfaces","Create one VLAN interface for all three","Put the parent in a virtual wire","Create three loopback interfaces"],0,"Layer 3 subinterfaces (for example ethernet1/3.10) each take a VLAN tag, an IP address and their own zone. A single VLAN interface would not separate them into different zones.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — interfaces",[null,"A VLAN interface gives one Layer 2 VLAN an IP address and sits in one zone, so it cannot route three VLANs into three separate zones.","A virtual wire has no IP addresses and does not route, so it cannot provide routed gateways for the three VLANs.","Loopbacks are always-up logical interfaces with no link to the physical trunk; they cannot receive 802.1Q-tagged traffic."],2],["pa7",0,1,"Users report they can reach the firewall's inside IP but pings to it fail, while GUI access works from the management port. What controls which services answer on a dataplane interface?",["A zone protection profile","A service route for management traffic","An SSL/TLS service profile","An interface management profile"],3,"Interface management profiles allow ping, SSH, HTTPS, response pages and other services on dataplane interfaces. Zone protection defends against floods and does not grant services.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — interfaces",["A zone protection profile defends the zone against floods, reconnaissance and malformed packets; it never grants services such as ping on an interface.","A service route picks which interface the firewall uses to source its own management services (updates, DNS, syslog); it does not permit inbound ping.","An SSL/TLS service profile sets the certificate and TLS versions for services like the GUI or GlobalProtect portal, not whether ping is answered.",null],2],["pa8",0,1,"An engineer needs the firewall to answer DHCP requests for a new guest VLAN whose clients have no other DHCP server. What should be configured on the guest subinterface?",["A DHCP relay","A DHCP server","A DNS proxy","A DHCP client"],1,"The DHCP server option hands out leases from a pool the firewall owns. A relay only forwards requests to another server, which the guest VLAN does not have.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — interfaces",["A DHCP relay only forwards requests to another DHCP server, and the guest VLAN has no other server to relay to.",null,"A DNS proxy answers or forwards DNS queries; it does not hand out IP address leases.","A DHCP client makes the firewall interface obtain its own address from a server; it does not serve leases to guest clients."],1],["pa9",0,1,"A firewall learns 10.1.0.0/16 through OSPF (internal) and also has a static route to the same prefix, both using default settings. Which route is installed?",["The OSPF route, administrative distance 30","The static route, administrative distance 10","The OSPF route, lower metric","Both, installed as equal-cost ECMP paths"],1,"PAN-OS default administrative distances are 10 for static routes and 30 for OSPF internal; the lower value wins. ECMP applies only to equal-cost paths from the same selection.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — routing",["OSPF internal routes have a default administrative distance of 30, which is higher (less preferred) than the static route's 10.",null,"Metric is only compared between routes from the same source; between different protocols the lower administrative distance wins first.","ECMP only installs multiple equal-cost paths; routes with different administrative distances are not equal, so only the best one is installed."],3],["pa10",0,1,"Which PAN-OS default administrative distance applies to eBGP routes?",["200","110","120","20"],3,"By default eBGP is 20 and iBGP is 200. 110 is OSPF external and 120 is RIP.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — routing",["200 is the default for iBGP routes, not eBGP.","110 is the PAN-OS default for OSPF external routes, not eBGP.","120 is the default administrative distance for RIP, not eBGP.",null],1],["pa11",0,1,"A company wants all traffic from the finance subnet to use a second ISP regardless of the routing table, with fallback if that ISP fails. What should be configured?",["Policy-based forwarding with path monitoring","A second default route with lower metric","Destination NAT to the second ISP","A virtual wire interface on the second ISP link pair"],0,"PBF rules steer matching traffic to a chosen egress and next hop ahead of the routing table, and monitoring can disable the rule so traffic falls back. A second default route would affect every subnet.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — routing",[null,"A second default route with a better metric would change the path for every subnet, not just finance, and cannot select traffic by source.","Destination NAT rewrites where traffic is going; it does not choose the egress ISP link for outbound finance traffic.","A virtual wire is a transparent bump in the wire with no routing, so it cannot steer one subnet toward a chosen ISP with failover."],2],["pa12",0,1,"On PAN-OS 10.2 and later, which feature must be enabled to configure logical routers instead of legacy virtual routers?",["Multi-virtual system mode","ECMP","Advanced Routing Engine","SD-WAN plugin"],2,"Logical routers belong to the Advanced Routing Engine, enabled under Device > Setup > Management and then committed and rebooted. Multi-vsys is unrelated to the routing engine.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — routing",["Multi-vsys mode splits the firewall into virtual systems; it has nothing to do with enabling logical routers.","ECMP is a routing option available on both engines for equal-cost paths; it does not switch the firewall to logical routers.",null,"The SD-WAN plugin adds SD-WAN path selection features; it is not what enables logical routers instead of legacy virtual routers."],1],["pa13",0,1,"An engineer wants traffic to two ISPs with equal routes to be shared across both links. Which feature should be enabled on the router?",["Policy-based forwarding","Route redistribution","Source NAT","ECMP"],3,"Equal-cost multipath installs several equal routes and spreads sessions across them using a load-balancing method. PBF steers selected traffic rather than balancing.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — routing",["PBF forces selected traffic to a specific egress and next hop; it steers traffic rather than balancing sessions across equal routes.","Redistribution imports routes from one protocol into another; it does not spread sessions across two equal paths.","Source NAT translates addresses; it does not make the router use two equal routes at once.",null],1],["pa14",0,1,"A PAN-OS firewall must advertise its internal networks to two ISPs and receive default routes from both. Which routing protocol is designed for this?",["OSPF","BGP","RIP","Static routes"],1,"BGP is the inter-domain protocol used to exchange routes with ISPs and apply per-peer policy. OSPF is an interior protocol for a single organization.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — routing",["OSPF is an interior gateway protocol for routing within one organization; ISPs exchange routes with customers using BGP.",null,"RIP is a simple distance-vector interior protocol and is not used for route exchange with ISPs.","Static routes cannot advertise internal networks to the ISPs or dynamically receive their default routes with per-peer policy."],2],["pa15",0,1,"Two firewalls are being paired in active/passive HA. Which link carries hellos, heartbeats and configuration synchronization?",["HA2 data link","HA3 packet-forwarding link","HA1 control link","The management service route"],2,"HA1 is the control link for hellos, heartbeats, HA state and config sync. HA2 synchronizes sessions, forwarding tables and IPsec SAs.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — high availability",["HA2 carries session, forwarding table and IPsec SA synchronization, not hellos, heartbeats or config sync.","HA3 exists only in active/active and forwards packets to the session owner; it does not carry control traffic.",null,"A service route sets the source interface for the firewall's own services; it is not an HA link and does not carry heartbeats."],1],["pa16",0,1,"After an active/passive failover, existing user sessions dropped even though the passive firewall took over. Which HA link most likely was not working?",["HA2","HA1","HA1 backup","HA3"],0,"Session state is synchronized over HA2; without it the new active peer has no session table and flows must restart. HA1 would affect election and config sync.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — high availability",[null,"HA1 carries hellos, heartbeats and config sync; since the passive peer took over correctly, election worked and HA1 was not the cause of dropped sessions.","The HA1 backup link only provides redundancy for control traffic; it does not synchronize session state.","HA3 is used only in active/active for packet forwarding; an active/passive pair does not use it for session sync."],2],["pa17",0,1,"Which HA link exists only in active/active deployments and forwards packets to the peer that owns a session?",["HA3","HA2","HA1","HA4"],0,"HA3 is a Layer 2 link used in active/active to send packets to the peer that owns the session for asymmetric flows. HA2 syncs session state in both modes.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — high availability",[null,"HA2 synchronizes session state and forwarding tables in both active/passive and active/active modes; it does not forward packets to the owner.","HA1 is the control link used in both modes for hellos, heartbeats and config sync.","PAN-OS has HA4 only for firewall clustering on specific platforms; it is not the active/active packet-forwarding link."],1],["pa18",0,1,"Firewall A (priority 50) and Firewall B (priority 100) are in active/passive HA. B became active while A rebooted. The team wants A to become active again automatically. What must be set?",["Preemptive on B only","A higher priority number on A","Preemptive on both firewalls","Passive link state set to auto"],2,"Preemption must be enabled on both peers, and the lower priority number (A) is preferred. A higher number would make A less preferred.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — high availability",["If only B has preemption enabled, A will not reclaim the active role; preemption must be enabled on both peers.","A higher priority number makes a device less preferred, so this would make A even less likely to become active.",null,"Passive link state controls whether the passive peer's links stay up; it does not make a preferred peer retake the active role."],3],["pa19",0,1,"An engineer wants the active firewall to fail over if its upstream router at 203.0.113.1 stops responding, even though the local link is still up. What should be configured?",["HA link monitoring","HA path monitoring","Zone protection","Tunnel monitoring"],1,"Path monitoring pings a destination and fails over when it becomes unreachable. Link monitoring only watches physical interface state, which is still up here.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — high availability",["Link monitoring only watches the physical state of interfaces, and the local link here is still up, so it would not trigger failover.",null,"Zone protection defends zones against floods and reconnaissance; it does not monitor upstream reachability or trigger HA failover.","Tunnel monitoring checks an IPsec peer or remote IP through a VPN tunnel; it is not the HA mechanism for failing over when a router stops responding."],2],["pa20",0,1,"Which requirement applies to both members of a PAN-OS HA pair?",["Same hostname and management IP","Same serial number","Different content versions","Same model and PAN-OS version"],3,"HA peers must be the same model running the same PAN-OS version, with matching licenses and content. Hostnames and management IPs must be unique.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — high availability",["Hostnames and management IPs must be unique on each peer; identical values would cause conflicts.","Every firewall has its own unique serial number, so two peers can never share one.","Peers should run matching content versions; mismatched content can break HA sync and session handling.",null],1],["pa21",0,1,"In an active/active pair, the team needs one gateway IP that both firewalls can answer for so hosts need no changes on failover. What provides this?",["Service route","Loopback per firewall","Floating IP address","Static ARP entry on each host"],2,"Floating IPs in active/active are bound to a device ID and move to the peer on failure; ARP load-sharing is the related option. Static ARP on hosts would break on failover.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — high availability",["A service route selects the source interface for the firewall's own services; it does not provide a shared gateway IP for hosts.","A loopback on each firewall gives each device its own address, which would not move to the peer when one fails.",null,"Static ARP entries on hosts would pin the gateway to one MAC and break on failover, and they require changing every host."],2],["pa22",0,1,"A new site-to-site IPsec tunnel to a peer that uses policy-based VPN comes up in phase 1 but phase 2 fails with a mismatched traffic selector. What should be added on the PAN-OS side?",["Proxy IDs matching the peer's local and remote subnets","A second IKE gateway","A GRE tunnel carried inside the IPsec tunnel to the peer","Tunnel monitoring on the peer IP"],0,"Route-based PAN-OS sends 0.0.0.0/0 selectors by default; policy-based peers need proxy IDs that match their crypto ACLs. Monitoring would not fix a selector mismatch.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — IPsec",[null,"A second IKE gateway would not help, because phase 1 already succeeds; the failure is the phase 2 traffic selector mismatch.","A GRE tunnel inside IPsec changes the encapsulation but does not make the PAN-OS proxy IDs match the peer's crypto ACLs.","Tunnel monitoring only detects whether traffic passes through the tunnel; it cannot fix a selector mismatch that prevents phase 2 from coming up."],3],["pa23",0,1,"Which object holds the phase 1 encryption, authentication, DH group and lifetime settings for a PAN-OS site-to-site VPN?",["IPsec crypto profile","Tunnel interface","Certificate profile","IKE crypto profile"],3,"The IKE crypto profile defines phase 1 proposals and is referenced by the IKE gateway. The IPsec crypto profile covers phase 2.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — IPsec",["The IPsec crypto profile holds phase 2 (ESP/AH) proposals, not the phase 1 IKE settings.","A tunnel interface is the logical endpoint that routes point to; it holds no encryption or DH settings.","A certificate profile defines trusted CAs and revocation checks for certificate authentication, not phase 1 proposals.",null],1],["pa24",0,1,"An IPsec tunnel is up, but no traffic crosses it. The tunnel interface is in a zone and security rules exist. What else is required for a route-based VPN?",["A proxy ID for every host","A route pointing the remote subnet at the tunnel interface","A NAT rule for the tunnel","A decryption policy that decrypts the ESP traffic in the tunnel"],1,"PAN-OS VPNs are route-based: traffic enters the tunnel only when a route sends the remote networks to the tunnel interface. Proxy IDs are for policy-based peers, not host lists.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — IPsec",["Proxy IDs are only needed to match policy-based peers' selectors, and they are defined per subnet pair, not for every host.",null,"A NAT rule is not required for site-to-site traffic between private networks and would not make traffic enter the tunnel.","Decryption policy applies to SSL/SSH sessions; ESP cannot be decrypted by it and it plays no part in sending traffic into a tunnel."],3],["pa25",0,1,"An organization worried about harvest-now-decrypt-later attacks wants IKEv2 tunnels that resist future quantum computers. Which PAN-OS capability addresses this?",["Switching phase 2 to AES-128","Quantum-resistant IKEv2 with post-quantum preshared keys","Using IKEv1 aggressive mode","Replacing IPsec with an unencrypted GRE tunnel to each peer"],1,"PAN-OS 11.1 and later support quantum-resistant IKEv2 VPNs that mix a post-quantum preshared key (RFC 8784) into key derivation, and newer releases add hybrid key exchanges (RFC 9370). GRE adds no encryption at all.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — IPsec and quantum-resistant cryptography",["AES-128 is a classical symmetric cipher choice; it does nothing to protect the IKE key exchange against quantum attacks.",null,"IKEv1 aggressive mode is an older, weaker exchange that exposes identity information; it adds no quantum resistance.","GRE provides no encryption at all, so replacing IPsec with it would expose traffic rather than protect it."],2],["pa26",0,1,"A partner router needs a lightweight tunnel to carry multicast routing protocol traffic, and encryption is handled elsewhere. Which tunnel type fits?",["GlobalProtect tunnel","SSL decryption tunnel","Tap interface","GRE tunnel"],3,"PAN-OS can terminate GRE tunnels with keepalives; GRE carries routing protocols and multicast but provides no encryption. GlobalProtect is for remote users.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — GRE tunnels",["GlobalProtect tunnels connect remote users or satellites; they are not a lightweight router-to-router tunnel for multicast routing protocols.","There is no SSL decryption tunnel for carrying traffic; SSL decryption inspects sessions, it does not build tunnels to partners.","A tap interface passively receives mirrored traffic and cannot build a tunnel to a partner router.",null],2],["pa27",0,1,"In GlobalProtect, which component gives the app its configuration and the list of gateways to connect to?",["Portal","Gateway","HIP profile","Clientless VPN"],0,"The portal authenticates the user and delivers the agent configuration, including gateways. Gateways terminate the tunnels and enforce policy.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — GlobalProtect",[null,"Gateways terminate the tunnels and enforce policy; they do not hand out the app configuration and gateway list.","A HIP profile matches endpoint posture data in policy; it does not deliver configuration to the app.","Clientless VPN gives browser access to published web apps without the app; it does not configure the GlobalProtect app."],1],["pa28",0,1,"Remote laptops must connect to GlobalProtect before the user signs in so that login scripts and drive mappings run. Which connect method supports this?",["On-demand","Clientless VPN","Pre-logon","User-logon with manual start"],2,"Pre-logon uses a machine certificate to establish the tunnel before Windows sign-in. On-demand waits for the user to connect.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — GlobalProtect",["On-demand waits for the user to manually connect, so the tunnel would not exist before Windows sign-in.","Clientless VPN is browser-based access to web apps and does not run before login or support login scripts and drive mappings.",null,"User-logon connects only after the user signs in to Windows, so login scripts would already have run without the tunnel."],2],["pa29",0,1,"Security wants remote users blocked from sensitive apps unless their disk is encrypted and endpoint protection is running. What GlobalProtect feature enables this?",["A split-tunnel exclude route","An external gateway priority","An agent upgrade setting on the GlobalProtect portal config","HIP objects and profiles used in security rules"],3,"Host information profile (HIP) checks report posture; HIP profiles can be matched in security policy. Split-tunnel only changes which traffic goes through the tunnel.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — GlobalProtect",["Split-tunnel exclude routes decide which traffic bypasses the tunnel; they do not check disk encryption or endpoint protection.","Gateway priority only affects which external gateway the app prefers; it does not assess endpoint posture.","Agent upgrade settings control how the app is updated; they do not block access based on posture.",null],2],["pa30",0,1,"A GlobalProtect deployment must send traffic for a video-conferencing SaaS directly to the internet while all other traffic goes through the tunnel. What should be configured?",["A second portal","Split tunnel exclusions on the gateway","An internal gateway","Tunnel monitoring"],1,"Split tunneling lets the gateway include or exclude routes, domains or applications from the tunnel. An internal gateway is used when users are on the corporate network.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — GlobalProtect",["A second portal would just deliver another configuration; it does not send one SaaS app's traffic outside the tunnel.",null,"An internal gateway is used when users are on the corporate network, not to route a SaaS app directly to the internet.","Tunnel monitoring checks site-to-site IPsec tunnel health; it has no role in GlobalProtect split tunneling."],2],["pa31",0,1,"When GlobalProtect cannot use IPsec because UDP 4501 is blocked on a hotel network, what does the app do by default?",["Disconnects until IPsec is allowed","Switches to GRE","Falls back to an SSL tunnel","Uses clientless VPN"],2,"GlobalProtect prefers IPsec and falls back to SSL over TCP 443 when IPsec is enabled but cannot connect. Clientless VPN is a separate browser-based feature.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — GlobalProtect",["The app does not stay disconnected; when IPsec cannot be established it automatically tries SSL.","GlobalProtect does not use GRE for its tunnels; its only tunnel types are IPsec and SSL.",null,"Clientless VPN is a separate browser-based feature, not the app's fallback when IPsec is blocked."],2],["pa32",0,1,"Many SYN packets from spoofed sources are flooding a DMZ web zone. Which PAN-OS feature protects the whole ingress zone with SYN cookies and flood thresholds?",["Zone protection profile","Vulnerability protection profile","File blocking profile","Interface management profile"],0,"Zone protection profiles apply flood, reconnaissance and packet-based protections to the ingress zone. Vulnerability profiles inspect allowed sessions for exploits, not floods.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — zones",[null,"A vulnerability protection profile inspects allowed sessions for exploit signatures; it does not apply SYN cookies or flood thresholds to a zone.","File blocking profiles control file types in allowed sessions and have no flood protection.","An interface management profile allows services like ping or SSH on an interface; it provides no flood defense."],2],["pa33",0,1,"An internal client uses a public DNS name that resolves to the firewall's public IP, which destination-NATs to a server in the same internal zone. The connection fails. Which fix is standard?",["Add U-turn NAT that also source-NATs the client","Put the server in a virtual wire","Add a second default route","Disable the intrazone-default rule for the internal zone"],0,"U-turn NAT needs destination NAT plus source NAT so return traffic comes back through the firewall. Changing interface types does not fix the asymmetric path.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — NAT",[null,"A virtual wire has no IP addressing or NAT for this design, so it would not fix the asymmetric return path between client and server.","A second default route does not change the fact that the server replies directly to the client, bypassing the firewall.","The intrazone-default rule allows same-zone traffic; disabling or changing it does not fix the missing source NAT."],3],["pa34",0,1,"When writing a security rule for inbound traffic that is destination-NATed from a public IP to a DMZ server, which zone and address should the rule use?",["Pre-NAT zone and post-NAT private IP","Post-NAT zone and post-NAT private IP","Post-NAT zone and pre-NAT public IP","Pre-NAT zone and pre-NAT public IP"],2,"Security policy matches the pre-NAT IP but the post-NAT zone, which the NAT lookup determined. Using the private IP is the most common mistake.","Domain 1: PAN-OS networking — NAT",["Security rules use the post-NAT zone, not the pre-NAT zone, and the pre-NAT public IP rather than the private IP.","The zone is right, but security rules match the original (pre-NAT) public IP, not the translated private IP.",null,"The address is right, but the zone must be the post-NAT zone that the route lookup found for the server, not the pre-NAT zone."],3],["pa35",0,2,"A junior admin must be able to view the configuration and logs but never commit changes. What is the simplest option?",["An authentication sequence","The superuser (read-only) dynamic role","A custom zone protection profile","The device administrator role"],1,"Dynamic roles such as superuser (read-only) are built in and update automatically with new features. Device administrator can make and commit changes.","Domain 2: device settings — admin roles",["An authentication sequence tries authentication profiles in order; it grants no permissions and does not prevent commits.",null,"A zone protection profile defends zones against floods and scans; it has nothing to do with administrator permissions.","The device administrator role can change the configuration and commit, which the junior admin must not do."],2],["pa36",0,2,"An admin needs to edit NAT rules but must not see the Device tab. What should be created?",["A new dynamic role","An authentication profile","A certificate profile","A role-based admin role profile"],3,"Admin role profiles let you enable or disable individual tabs, nodes and XML/REST API access. Dynamic roles are fixed and cannot be customized.","Domain 2: device settings — admin roles",["Dynamic roles are predefined and cannot be customized to hide a specific tab such as Device.","An authentication profile decides how an admin logs in, not which tabs they can see or edit.","A certificate profile validates certificates for authentication; it does not control GUI tabs or permissions.",null],2],["pa37",0,2,"Admins should authenticate with RADIUS, but if the RADIUS servers are unreachable they must still be able to log in with local accounts. What is configured?",["Two server profiles in one admin role","A certificate profile with OCSP","An authentication sequence of RADIUS then local","A second management interface with its own local admin"],2,"An authentication sequence tries authentication profiles in order, so a local database profile can follow RADIUS. Server profiles only list servers for one method.","Domain 2: device settings — authentication",["An admin role profile defines permissions only; it does not hold server profiles or provide fallback authentication.","A certificate profile validates client certificates and revocation; it does not fall back from RADIUS to local accounts.",null,"A second management interface does not provide authentication fallback; the fallback is configured through an authentication sequence."],2],["pa38",0,2,"Which object tells PAN-OS the addresses, ports and shared secret of the TACACS+ servers used for admin login?",["Server profile","Authentication policy rule","Admin role profile","Log forwarding profile"],0,"A server profile describes how to reach LDAP, RADIUS, TACACS+, Kerberos or SAML IdPs. An authentication profile then references it.","Domain 2: device settings — authentication",[null,"An authentication policy rule decides which user traffic must authenticate through Authentication Portal; it does not list TACACS+ servers.","An admin role profile sets what an admin may do after login, not how to reach TACACS+ servers.","A log forwarding profile sends logs to external servers; it does not define authentication servers or secrets."],1],["pa39",0,2,"Users must complete MFA before reaching a sensitive internal app, even if they are already mapped by User-ID. Which PAN-OS feature enforces this?",["A HIP profile","An admin role profile","A DoS protection policy with a classified profile for the app server","Authentication policy with an authentication enforcement object"],3,"Authentication policy redirects matching traffic to Authentication Portal and can require MFA through an authentication enforcement object. HIP checks device posture, not identity.","Domain 2: device settings — authentication",["A HIP profile checks device posture such as encryption and antivirus; it does not require the user to complete MFA.","An admin role profile controls firewall administrator permissions, not end-user access to internal apps.","DoS protection policy limits session rates to protect servers; it does not authenticate users or require MFA.",null],3],["pa40",0,2,"An MSSP wants separate policies, zones and administrators for three customers on one physical firewall. What should they configure?",["Three template stacks","Multiple virtual systems","Three dynamic admin roles","Three aggregate groups"],1,"Virtual systems (vsys) split one firewall into separate logical firewalls with their own zones, policies and admins. Template stacks are a Panorama feature.","Domain 2: device settings — virtual systems",["Template stacks are a Panorama feature for pushing device settings; they do not split one firewall into separate logical firewalls.",null,"Dynamic admin roles are fixed permission sets; they cannot give each customer separate zones and policies.","Aggregate groups bundle physical ports into one link and do nothing to separate customers."],2],["pa41",0,2,"Two vsys on one firewall need to share a single internet-facing interface and public IP. What is the intended feature?",["Inter-vsys external zone only","Shared gateway","Separate loopback per vsys","A virtual wire"],1,"A shared gateway is a special virtual system that lets multiple vsys use a common external interface. External zones allow vsys-to-vsys traffic but do not share an internet link.","Domain 2: device settings — virtual systems",["External zones let traffic pass between vsys, but on their own they do not let several vsys share one internet-facing interface and public IP.",null,"A loopback per vsys gives each its own logical address but does not provide a common physical uplink and public IP.","A virtual wire transparently bridges two ports without IP addressing, so it cannot act as a shared routed internet gateway."],2],["pa42",0,2,"Traffic must pass from vsys1 to vsys2 on the same firewall and be inspected in both. What must each vsys use?",["A tunnel interface","A tap zone","A HA3 link","An external zone referencing the other vsys"],3,"Inter-vsys traffic uses external zones, and each vsys needs security rules allowing it. Tap zones cannot forward traffic.","Domain 2: device settings — virtual systems",["Tunnel interfaces terminate VPNs such as IPsec or GRE; they are not how two vsys on the same box pass traffic.","A tap zone only receives mirrored traffic and cannot forward traffic between virtual systems.","HA3 is an active/active HA link between two firewalls; it has nothing to do with vsys-to-vsys traffic.",null],3],["pa43",0,2,"Security rules are allowing traffic, but no traffic logs reach the company syslog server. The syslog server profile exists. What is most likely missing?",["A log forwarding profile on the rules","A decryption profile","A zone protection profile","An HA link"],0,"Traffic and threat logs are sent off-box by a log forwarding profile attached to each rule. System and configuration logs are forwarded from Device > Log Settings.","Domain 2: device settings — logging",[null,"A decryption profile controls SSL checks for decrypted sessions; it does not send logs to syslog.","A zone protection profile defends against floods and scans and has no role in forwarding logs.","HA links synchronize state between peers; they do not forward traffic logs to an external syslog server."],2],["pa44",0,2,"By default, when does a PAN-OS firewall write a traffic log entry for an allowed session?",["At session start","Every 60 seconds","At session end","Only when a threat is found"],2,"Log at Session End is on by default. Log at Session Start is off by default because it creates many extra entries.","Domain 2: device settings — logging",["Log at Session Start is disabled by default because it generates many extra entries; it is not the default behavior.","PAN-OS does not write traffic logs on a fixed 60-second timer by default; the entry is written when the session ends.",null,"Traffic logs are written for allowed sessions regardless of threats; threat detections go to the separate threat log."],1],["pa45",0,2,"A company wants firewall logs stored in Palo Alto's cloud for analysis by other cloud apps instead of on local Log Collectors. What service is this?",["WildFire","Cloud Identity Engine","DNS Security","Strata Logging Service"],3,"Strata Logging Service (formerly Cortex Data Lake) is the cloud log store. Cloud Identity Engine provides user and group information.","Domain 2: device settings — logging",["WildFire analyzes unknown files and links for malware; it is not the cloud log storage service.","Cloud Identity Engine provides user and group information from directories; it does not store firewall logs.","DNS Security is a subscription that blocks malicious domains; it does not store firewall logs for other apps.",null],2],["pa46",0,2,"Which log type records an administrator's commit of a new security rule?",["Traffic log","Configuration log","Threat log","HIP match log"],1,"Configuration logs record changes admins make, and system logs record events such as commits starting and completing. Traffic logs describe sessions.","Domain 2: device settings — logging",["Traffic logs describe network sessions passing through the firewall, not administrator configuration changes.",null,"Threat logs record detections such as exploits, viruses or spyware, not admin changes.","HIP match logs record GlobalProtect endpoints matching HIP objects and profiles, not commits of rules."],1],["pa47",0,2,"A firewall on PAN-OS 10.2 must move to 11.1. What must be true before installing the 11.1 maintenance release?",["HA is disabled on both peers","All content updates are uninstalled","The 11.1 base image is downloaded","The config is reset to defaults"],2,"PAN-OS requires the base image of a feature release to be downloaded before a later maintenance release of it can be installed, and upgrades follow the supported path. HA does not need to be disabled.","Domain 2: device settings — software updates",["HA does not need to be disabled; HA pairs are upgraded one peer at a time while staying paired.","Content updates should stay installed; newer PAN-OS may even require a minimum content version, so removing them is wrong.",null,"Upgrading never requires a factory reset; the existing configuration is migrated to the new release."],3],["pa48",0,2,"What is the recommended order when upgrading PAN-OS on an active/passive HA pair?",["Suspend and upgrade passive, fail over, then upgrade the other peer","Upgrade the active first, then passive","Upgrade both at once to keep versions equal","Break the HA pair, upgrade both firewalls, then rebuild HA from scratch"],0,"Upgrading the passive peer first, then failing over and upgrading the original active, keeps traffic flowing. Upgrading both at once causes an outage.","Domain 2: device settings — software updates",[null,"Upgrading the active peer first takes down the firewall carrying traffic and causes an avoidable interruption.","Upgrading both at once reboots both peers together, leaving nothing to pass traffic and causing an outage.","Breaking and rebuilding HA is unnecessary extra work and risk; PAN-OS supports upgrading HA peers in sequence."],3],["pa49",0,2,"New App-IDs occasionally change how existing rules match. The team wants content updates installed only after they have been public for a while. What should be set?",["A threshold on the dynamic update schedule","Log at session start","A shorter HA hello interval","A new server profile"],0,"The dynamic update threshold waits a set number of hours after release before installing, and new App-ID installs can be delayed for review. Logging settings do not delay updates.","Domain 2: device settings — software updates",[null,"Log at session start only changes when traffic logs are written; it has no effect on when content updates install.","The HA hello interval controls peer failure detection speed and is unrelated to content update timing.","A server profile defines how to reach external servers such as syslog or LDAP; it does not delay updates."],3],["pa50",0,2,"Which dynamic update provides new application signatures used by App-ID?",["Antivirus","WildFire","Applications and Threats","GlobalProtect data file"],2,"Applications and Threats content includes App-ID signatures and vulnerability signatures. Antivirus content carries malware signatures.","Domain 2: device settings — software updates",["Antivirus content carries malware signatures, not the application signatures App-ID uses.","WildFire updates deliver signatures for newly discovered malware, not App-ID application signatures.",null,"The GlobalProtect data file supplies endpoint vendor information for HIP checks, not App-ID signatures."],1],["pa51",0,2,"Before a major change, an admin wants a named copy of the running configuration that can be loaded later from the same firewall. What should they do?",["Run commit force","Save a named configuration snapshot","Export the tech support file","Clear the candidate config"],1,"Saving a named snapshot keeps a copy that can later be loaded and committed. A tech support file is for troubleshooting, not rollback.","Domain 2: device settings — configuration management",["Commit force pushes the candidate config to running even if unchanged; it does not create a named copy to restore later.",null,"A tech support file bundles logs and state for support troubleshooting; it is not loaded back as a configuration.","Clearing the candidate config would discard pending edits; it does not save a copy of the running configuration."],1],["pa52",0,2,"An admin edits rules in the web UI and closes the browser without committing. Where do the changes live?",["The running configuration","The HA peer's configuration","The saved snapshot","The candidate configuration"],3,"Edits go to the candidate configuration and only take effect after a commit makes them running. They are not synced to an HA peer until committed.","Domain 2: device settings — configuration management",["Changes are not applied to the running configuration until they are committed.","Nothing is synced to the HA peer until a commit occurs, and uncommitted edits stay local.","A saved snapshot only exists if the admin explicitly saves one; edits do not go there automatically.",null],2],["pa53",0,2,"For SSL forward proxy, which certificate does the firewall use to sign impersonated server certificates when the real site's certificate is trusted?",["Forward untrust certificate","Inbound inspection server certificate","Forward trust certificate","SSL/TLS service profile certificate"],2,"The forward trust CA signs certificates for sites with valid chains, and clients must trust it. The forward untrust certificate is used when the server's chain is invalid so users see a warning.","Domain 2: device settings — certificates",["The forward untrust certificate is used when the real server's chain is invalid, so users see a warning; it is not used for trusted sites.","An inbound inspection certificate is the internal server's own certificate used to decrypt inbound traffic, not to sign outbound impersonated certs.",null,"An SSL/TLS service profile names a certificate for services the firewall itself hosts, such as the GUI; it does not sign forward proxy certificates."],2],["pa54",0,2,"When the real server certificate is expired or untrusted during forward proxy decryption, which certificate should the firewall present so users still get a browser warning?",["Forward untrust certificate","Forward trust certificate","Root CA of a public provider","Management interface certificate"],0,"The forward untrust certificate is deliberately not trusted by clients so an invalid server keeps showing an error. Signing with forward trust would hide the problem.","Domain 2: device settings — certificates",[null,"Signing with forward trust would make an expired or untrusted site appear valid, hiding the problem from users.","The firewall cannot sign with a public CA's root because it never has that private key, and hiding errors would be the wrong result anyway.","The management interface certificate secures the admin GUI; it is not used to present certificates during forward proxy decryption."],2],["pa55",0,2,"The firewall must decrypt inbound HTTPS to an internal web server the company owns. What must be imported to the firewall?",["A forward trust CA","The client's certificate","A public root CA bundle","The server's certificate and private key"],3,"SSL inbound inspection uses the server's own certificate and key to decrypt. Forward trust is for outbound forward proxy.","Domain 2: device settings — certificates",["A forward trust CA is used for outbound SSL forward proxy, not for decrypting inbound traffic to your own server.","Inbound inspection does not need client certificates; it uses the server's certificate and key to decrypt.","A public root CA bundle only validates certificates and contains no private keys, so it cannot decrypt the server's traffic.",null],2],["pa56",0,2,"Which object controls the TLS versions and certificate used by the GlobalProtect portal or the management web interface?",["Certificate profile","SSL/TLS service profile","Decryption profile","IKE crypto profile"],1,"An SSL/TLS service profile names the certificate and minimum/maximum TLS versions for services the firewall hosts. Certificate profiles validate client or peer certificates.","Domain 2: device settings — certificates",["A certificate profile validates client or peer certificates; it does not set the TLS versions for services the firewall hosts.",null,"A decryption profile controls checks on sessions the firewall decrypts, not the TLS settings of the portal or web UI.","An IKE crypto profile holds IPsec phase 1 proposals and has nothing to do with TLS for the portal or management UI."],1],["pa57",0,2,"GlobalProtect must accept only devices with a certificate issued by the company CA and check revocation. What should be referenced on the portal and gateway?",["An SSL/TLS service profile","A certificate profile with OCSP or CRL","A decryption profile","A server profile"],1,"Certificate profiles define trusted CAs, username field and revocation checking for client authentication. SSL/TLS service profiles are for the server side.","Domain 2: device settings — certificates",["An SSL/TLS service profile configures the server certificate and TLS versions the portal presents; it does not validate client certificates.",null,"A decryption profile applies to decrypted user sessions and does not authenticate GlobalProtect clients.","A server profile points to servers such as LDAP or RADIUS; it does not define trusted CAs or revocation checks for client certificates."],2],["pa58",0,2,"Security rules use Active Directory groups, but the firewall must learn users and groups without on-premises LDAP connectivity from every site. Which Palo Alto cloud service provides this?",["Strata Logging Service","Advanced WildFire","Enterprise DLP","Cloud Identity Engine"],3,"Cloud Identity Engine syncs directories such as Microsoft Entra ID and on-premises AD to provide user and group data. Strata Logging Service stores logs.","Domain 2: device settings — Cloud Identity Engine",["Strata Logging Service stores firewall logs in the cloud; it does not supply user and group data.","Advanced WildFire analyzes unknown files for malware and provides no directory information.","Enterprise DLP detects sensitive data in traffic; it does not sync users and groups from directories.",null],3],["pa59",0,2,"The firewall shows IP addresses but no usernames for a new user subnet, though User-ID agents work elsewhere. What is a likely cause?",["User-ID is not enabled on the source zone","The zone has no zone protection profile","Log at session start is disabled","The HA2 link used for session synchronization is down"],0,"User-ID must be enabled on the zone where users originate, or mappings are not applied there. Zone protection has no effect on mapping.","Domain 2: device settings — User-ID",[null,"Zone protection profiles defend against floods and scans and have no effect on whether User-ID mappings apply.","Log at session start only changes logging timing; logs would show usernames if mapping were applied to the zone.","HA2 synchronizes sessions between peers and does not control whether a zone uses User-ID."],3],["pa60",0,2,"Which User-ID method reads Windows security logon events (such as event 4624) from domain controllers?",["Authentication Portal","XML API","Server monitoring","GlobalProtect"],2,"Server monitoring with the PAN-OS integrated or Windows User-ID agent reads DC security logs for logon events. Authentication Portal prompts the user directly.","Domain 2: device settings — User-ID",["Authentication Portal prompts users through a web form; it does not read domain controller logon events.","The XML API receives mappings pushed by external scripts or systems; it does not read DC security logs itself.",null,"GlobalProtect maps users when they connect with the app; it does not read Windows event 4624 from domain controllers."],1],["pa61",0,2,"A Linux-based wireless controller authenticates users but cannot run the User-ID agent. It can send syslog. How can the firewall learn mappings?",["Server monitoring of the controller","A certificate profile","Client probing with WMI","Syslog parse profiles on a syslog listener"],3,"The User-ID syslog listener uses parse profiles to extract user and IP from syslog messages. WMI probing works only on Windows clients.","Domain 2: device settings — User-ID",["Server monitoring reads Microsoft domain controller or Exchange logs; it cannot monitor a Linux wireless controller.","A certificate profile validates certificates for authentication and does not learn IP-to-user mappings from syslog.","WMI client probing queries Windows endpoints; it is not supported on this Linux controller and Palo Alto discourages it.",null],3],["pa62",0,2,"An admin wants to restrict management UI access to one admin subnet. Where is this set for the dedicated management port?",["A security rule in the untrust zone","Permitted IP addresses in management interface settings","A zone protection profile","A policy-based forwarding rule for the management subnet traffic"],1,"Permitted IP addresses on the management interface limit who can reach services on it. Security rules do not govern the out-of-band management port.","Domain 2: device settings — management",["Security rules apply to dataplane traffic and do not govern access to the out-of-band management port.",null,"A zone protection profile defends dataplane zones against floods and does not apply to the management port.","PBF steers transit traffic to a chosen next hop; it cannot restrict who reaches the management interface."],2],["pa63",0,2,"The management port has no internet access, but the firewall must download content updates through a dataplane interface. What should be configured?",["A PBF rule for the management IP","A tap interface","A service route for the update service","A DHCP relay"],2,"Service routes let services such as updates, DNS or syslog source from a dataplane interface instead of the management port. PBF does not apply to firewall-originated services.","Domain 2: device settings — management",["PBF applies to transit traffic entering dataplane interfaces, not to traffic the firewall itself originates for updates.","A tap interface only receives mirrored traffic and cannot send the firewall's update requests out.",null,"A DHCP relay forwards client DHCP requests to a server; it has nothing to do with downloading content updates."],2],["pa64",0,2,"Web browsers in a branch are configured with the firewall's IP as their proxy on port 8080. Which PAN-OS 11.x feature supports this model?",["Explicit web proxy","Transparent proxy","Virtual wire","Clientless VPN"],0,"With an explicit proxy, clients are configured to send requests to the proxy. A transparent proxy intercepts traffic without client configuration.","Domain 2: device settings — web proxy",[null,"A transparent proxy intercepts traffic without any client proxy settings, the opposite of browsers pointed at the firewall on port 8080.","A virtual wire is a transparent inline deployment with no IP address, so browsers could not be pointed at it as a proxy.","Clientless VPN publishes internal web apps through the GlobalProtect portal; it is not a forward web proxy for browsing."],2],["pa65",0,2,"Two admins edit the same firewall at once and one keeps overwriting the other's work. What PAN-OS feature prevents this?",["Configuration lock","Commit validation","Candidate config revert","Log forwarding"],0,"A config lock blocks other admins from changing the candidate configuration until it is released. Validation only checks syntax.","Domain 2: device settings — configuration management",[null,"Commit validation only checks the candidate configuration for errors; it does not stop another admin from editing it.","Reverting the candidate config discards changes after the fact rather than preventing simultaneous edits.","Log forwarding sends logs to external systems and has no effect on concurrent configuration edits."],1],["pa66",0,2,"An admin only wants to commit their own changes while a colleague's half-finished work stays uncommitted. What should they use?",["Commit force","Load named config","Partial commit of their changes","Revert to running"],2,"Commit changes made by a specific admin to push only those edits. Commit force pushes everything, including the colleague's work.","Domain 2: device settings — configuration management",["Commit force commits the entire candidate configuration, including the colleague's unfinished changes.","Loading a named config replaces the candidate with a saved configuration and does not isolate one admin's edits.",null,"Reverting to running discards all uncommitted changes, including the admin's own work, instead of committing them."],2],["pa67",0,2,"A decryption policy breaks a banking app that uses certificate pinning. What is the right fix?",["Replace forward trust with forward untrust","Add a no-decrypt rule for that app's domains","Disable the SSL/TLS service profile","Remove the certificate profile from every GlobalProtect gateway"],1,"Pinned apps reject the firewall's impersonated certificate, so they are added to a no-decrypt rule or the decryption exclusion list. Swapping to forward untrust would break every site.","Domain 2: device settings — certificates",["Using forward untrust for all sites would make every decrypted site show a browser warning, and the pinned app would still fail.",null,"The SSL/TLS service profile applies to services the firewall hosts, not to outbound decryption of the banking app.","Certificate profiles on GlobalProtect gateways validate client certificates and have nothing to do with the pinned app breaking."],2],["pa68",0,3,"In Panorama, where are security rules and address objects for a group of branch firewalls defined?",["Templates","Template stacks","Log collector groups","Device groups"],3,"Device groups hold policies and objects. Templates hold network and device settings such as interfaces, zones and server profiles.","Domain 3: integration and automation — Panorama",["Templates hold network and device settings such as interfaces and zones, not security rules and address objects.","Template stacks combine templates for device settings; they do not hold policy or objects.","Log Collector groups manage log storage and forwarding, not security policy.",null],1],["pa69",0,3,"A Panorama admin wants all branch firewalls to share the same DNS, NTP and syslog settings, with interfaces defined per region. What should be used?",["A single device group per firewall","Pre-rules in the shared device group","Template stack of a common template and regional templates","One separate template per firewall, with no template stack at all"],2,"Template stacks combine templates, with the higher template in the stack taking priority. Device groups are for policies and objects, not device settings.","Domain 3: integration and automation — Panorama",["Device groups hold policy and objects, not DNS, NTP or syslog settings, and one per firewall defeats shared management.","Pre-rules are security policy; DNS, NTP and syslog are device settings that belong in templates.",null,"A separate template per firewall without a stack forces admins to duplicate the common DNS, NTP and syslog settings everywhere."],3],["pa70",0,3,"A security rule must be evaluated before any rule a local branch admin writes on the firewall. Where should it be placed in Panorama?",["Pre-rules in a device group","Post-rules in a device group","The template","Local rules on each firewall"],0,"Panorama pre-rules are evaluated before local rules, and post-rules after them. Templates do not contain policy.","Domain 3: integration and automation — Panorama",[null,"Post-rules are evaluated after local firewall rules, so they cannot take precedence over a local admin's rules.","Templates carry network and device settings; they cannot contain security rules.","Local rules on each firewall are exactly what the rule must be evaluated before, and they are not managed centrally."],2],["pa71",0,3,"A branch firewall needs one template value (its hostname) different from what the Panorama template pushes. What is the preferred way?",["Create a new device group","Edit the shared pre-rules","Disconnect it from Panorama","Use a template variable or per-device override"],3,"Template variables and per-device values let one template serve many firewalls with unique settings. Device groups do not handle hostnames.","Domain 3: integration and automation — Panorama",["A device group holds policy and objects, not device settings such as the hostname.","Shared pre-rules are security policy and cannot set a firewall's hostname.","Disconnecting from Panorama removes central management entirely and is not a way to vary one setting.",null],2],["pa72",0,3,"After editing a device group in Panorama, the changes do not appear on the firewalls. What step is missing?",["Only save the Panorama config","Commit to Panorama, then push to devices","Reboot the firewalls","Refresh the license on each firewall"],1,"Panorama changes must be committed to Panorama and then pushed to managed devices. Saving only updates the Panorama candidate.","Domain 3: integration and automation — Panorama",["Saving only updates Panorama's candidate configuration; the change is neither committed on Panorama nor pushed to firewalls.",null,"Rebooting firewalls does not pull uncommitted Panorama changes; they must be pushed from Panorama.","Licenses have nothing to do with configuration delivery from Panorama."],2],["pa73",0,3,"A dedicated Panorama appliance should only receive and store logs for other Panoramas and never manage firewalls. Which mode is used?",["Panorama mode","Log Collector mode","Management Only mode","Legacy mode"],1,"Log Collector mode turns the appliance into a dedicated collector managed by another Panorama. Management Only mode manages devices but stores no firewall logs locally.","Domain 3: integration and automation — Panorama",["Panorama mode manages firewalls and can collect logs locally, which is broader than a log-only appliance.",null,"Management Only mode manages firewalls without storing their logs, the opposite of what is needed.","Legacy mode is an older mode for some Panorama virtual appliances and is not the dedicated log collector role."],1],["pa74",0,3,"Which is true about Panorama and managed firewall software versions?",["Firewalls must run a later release than Panorama","Versions must always be identical","Panorama cannot manage firewalls running different PAN-OS releases","Panorama must run the same or a later release than its firewalls"],3,"Panorama must run the same or a newer PAN-OS release than the firewalls it manages. It can manage firewalls on several older supported releases.","Domain 3: integration and automation — Panorama",["It is the other way around: Panorama must run the same or a newer release than the firewalls it manages.","Identical versions are not required; Panorama can manage firewalls running several older supported releases.","Panorama can manage firewalls on several different supported releases, as long as none is newer than Panorama.",null],2],["pa75",0,3,"A script needs to authenticate to the PAN-OS XML API. What is the first call it usually makes?",["Request with type=keygen to get an API key","Commit with type=commit","Export with type=export","Operational call show system info"],0,"type=keygen with username and password returns an API key used for later calls. Other calls need that key first.","Domain 3: integration and automation — APIs",[null,"type=commit activates the candidate configuration and itself requires an API key, so it cannot be the first authentication call.","type=export downloads files such as configurations or certificates and also needs an API key first.","An operational command such as show system info needs an API key, which must be obtained first with keygen."],1],["pa76",0,3,"An automation account should call the REST API to manage policies but must not log in to the web UI. What should be configured?",["The superuser dynamic role","A certificate profile","An admin role profile with only REST API permissions","An authentication sequence that tries local then RADIUS accounts"],2,"Admin role profiles can enable the XML or REST API while disabling the web UI and CLI. Superuser grants everything.","Domain 3: integration and automation — APIs",["The superuser role grants full access to the GUI, CLI and APIs, violating the requirement to block web UI logins.","A certificate profile validates certificates for authentication; it does not limit which interfaces an admin can use.",null,"An authentication sequence only controls where credentials are checked; it does not restrict the account to the REST API."],2],["pa77",0,3,"A team wants firewall rules and objects defined as code, kept in Git and applied with plan and apply steps. Which tool fits best?",["SNMP traps","Tech support files","Running the PAN-OS CLI debug commands","Terraform with the panos provider"],3,"The panos Terraform provider manages PAN-OS and Panorama configuration declaratively. SNMP is for monitoring only.","Domain 3: integration and automation — automation",["SNMP traps send monitoring alerts and cannot define rules and objects as code.","Tech support files are troubleshooting bundles, not a way to declare and apply configuration.","CLI debug commands are for troubleshooting and are not a declarative, Git-driven plan-and-apply workflow.",null],2],["pa78",0,3,"An engineer wants playbooks that configure address objects and rules on many firewalls with idempotent tasks. Which tool has an official Palo Alto collection?",["Wireshark","Ansible","Nmap","Syslog-ng"],1,"The paloaltonetworks.panos Ansible collection provides modules for objects, rules and commits. The others are not configuration tools.","Domain 3: integration and automation — automation",["Wireshark is a packet capture and analysis tool, not a configuration management tool.",null,"Nmap is a network scanner used for discovery; it does not configure firewalls.","Syslog-ng collects and forwards logs; it has no modules for configuring PAN-OS."],1],["pa79",0,3,"The SOC keeps a threat feed of malicious IPs on a web server that updates hourly. How should the firewall use it without admins editing rules?",["Static address group","Custom App-ID","External dynamic list referenced in a rule","A template variable in the Panorama template"],2,"An EDL is fetched on a schedule and can be used as a source or destination in policy with no commit when it changes. Static groups require manual edits.","Domain 3: integration and automation — integration",["A static address group requires admins to edit members and commit each time the feed changes.","A custom App-ID identifies applications by signatures; it is not a list of malicious IP addresses.",null,"Template variables supply per-device values for network and device settings, not hourly-changing threat IP lists in policy."],2],["pa80",0,3,"Servers get tagged 'quarantine' by an automation tool and must immediately be blocked. Which object makes this work without a commit?",["Dynamic address group matching the tag","Static address object","Service group","Application filter"],0,"Dynamic address groups resolve membership from tags registered by the API, VM information sources or auto-tagging, so rules update without a commit. Static objects need edits and commits.","Domain 3: integration and automation — integration",[null,"A static address object must be edited and committed to change, so it cannot block a newly tagged server immediately.","Service groups bundle ports and protocols, not tagged IP addresses.","Application filters group applications by attributes like category or risk, not hosts by tag."],3],["pa81",0,3,"The team wants a host to be tagged automatically when a threat log shows it contacting a command-and-control server. What PAN-OS feature does this?",["Auto-tagging action in a log forwarding profile","Zone protection profile","Service route","An aggregate Ethernet interface group with LACP"],0,"Log forwarding profiles can add built-in actions that tag the source or destination IP, which feeds dynamic address groups. Zone protection handles floods.","Domain 3: integration and automation — integration",[null,"Zone protection defends against floods and scans; it cannot tag an IP based on a threat log.","A service route sets the source interface for the firewall's own services and does not tag hosts.","An aggregate Ethernet group bundles physical ports and has nothing to do with tagging hosts from logs."],2],["pa82",0,3,"Firewall threat logs must open tickets in an ITSM tool through its web API. Which server profile supports this?",["Syslog server profile","SNMP trap profile","HTTP server profile","Kerberos server profile"],2,"HTTP server profiles send logs as HTTP requests with custom payloads to web services. Syslog sends plain syslog messages.","Domain 3: integration and automation — integration",["A syslog server profile sends standard syslog messages; it cannot call a ticketing system's web API with a custom payload.","SNMP traps send monitoring notifications to an SNMP manager, not HTTP requests to an ITSM API.",null,"A Kerberos server profile is used for authentication; it does not forward logs anywhere."],1],["pa83",0,3,"A new VM-Series firewall in the cloud must come up with its license, content and base configuration without anyone logging in. What should be used?",["A config lock","A bootstrap package","A template variable only","A named snapshot on another firewall"],1,"A bootstrap package (config, content, license, software and plugins folders, plus init-cfg.txt) is read on first boot from a storage bucket or ISO. A snapshot would need manual loading.","Domain 3: integration and automation — bootstrapping",["A config lock stops other admins from editing; it does nothing to provision a new firewall on first boot.",null,"A template variable only supplies per-device values once the firewall is managed by Panorama; it cannot license or configure a fresh VM alone.","A snapshot from another firewall must be manually imported and loaded, which requires someone to log in."],2],["pa84",0,3,"In a VM-Series bootstrap package, which file supplies the Panorama server IP, auth key and template stack for first boot?",["bootstrap.xml","authcodes","content folder","init-cfg.txt"],3,"init-cfg.txt holds basic settings such as hostname, Panorama server, auth key, device group and template stack. bootstrap.xml holds a full config.","Domain 3: integration and automation — bootstrapping",["bootstrap.xml holds a complete firewall configuration, not the basic Panorama registration settings.","The authcodes file (in the license folder) contains license auth codes, not Panorama details.","The content folder holds content update packages such as Applications and Threats, not Panorama settings.",null],2],["pa85",0,3,"A new branch firewall ships directly to a site with no IT staff. It should reach Panorama automatically when cabled to the internet. Which feature supports this?",["Clientless VPN","Tap mode","Zero Touch Provisioning","Log Collector mode"],2,"ZTP firewalls register through the ZTP service and connect to Panorama with no local setup. Log Collector mode applies to Panorama appliances.","Domain 3: integration and automation — deployment",["Clientless VPN gives remote users browser access to web apps; it does not provision new firewalls.","Tap mode passively monitors mirrored traffic and has no role in onboarding a firewall to Panorama.",null,"Log Collector mode is a Panorama appliance role for storing logs, not a way to provision branch firewalls."],2],["pa86",0,3,"A company wants to protect workloads in an AWS VPC with a Palo Alto firewall delivered as a managed native service, without running VM instances. What fits?",["Cloud NGFW for AWS","CN-Series","PA-400 series","Tap interface"],0,"Cloud NGFW for AWS is a managed firewall service; VM-Series is self-managed on instances. CN-Series runs inside Kubernetes clusters.","Domain 3: integration and automation — deployment options",[null,"CN-Series is a containerized firewall for Kubernetes clusters, not a managed native AWS service for VPCs.","The PA-400 series is a physical appliance for small offices and cannot be deployed inside an AWS VPC.","A tap interface is a passive monitoring mode on a firewall, not a cloud firewall service."],2],["pa87",0,3,"A platform team needs Layer 7 inspection of traffic between pods inside a Kubernetes cluster. Which Palo Alto form factor is designed for this?",["PA-Series appliance","Cloud NGFW for Azure","Panorama Log Collector","CN-Series"],3,"CN-Series is the containerized firewall deployed in Kubernetes. A hardware appliance cannot see pod-to-pod traffic inside a node.","Domain 3: integration and automation — deployment options",["A PA-Series hardware appliance sits outside the cluster and cannot see pod-to-pod traffic within a node.","Cloud NGFW for Azure protects Azure virtual networks as a managed service; it is not deployed inside Kubernetes to inspect pod traffic.","A Panorama Log Collector stores logs and does not inspect any traffic.",null],2],["pa88",0,3,"Which cloud-delivered product lets admins manage NGFWs and Prisma Access from one console with AIOps-based best practice checks?",["Cloud Identity Engine","Strata Cloud Manager","Expedition","GlobalProtect portal"],1,"Strata Cloud Manager provides unified cloud management, visibility and AIOps checks. Cloud Identity Engine is only for identity.","Domain 3: integration and automation — management platforms",["Cloud Identity Engine only provides user and group identity data; it is not a management console.",null,"Expedition is a migration and configuration optimization tool, not a unified cloud console for NGFWs and Prisma Access.","The GlobalProtect portal configures remote access clients; it does not manage firewalls."],1],["pa89",0,3,"A Python developer wants object-oriented access to PAN-OS and Panorama configuration instead of building raw XML calls. Which open-source SDK is intended for this?",["pandas","pan-os-python","paramiko","scapy"],1,"pan-os-python models firewalls, Panorama, objects and policies as Python classes over the API. Paramiko is a generic SSH library.","Domain 3: integration and automation — automation",["pandas is a general data analysis library with no knowledge of PAN-OS objects or APIs.",null,"Paramiko is a generic SSH library, so using it would mean scripting CLI sessions, not object-oriented config access.","Scapy crafts and analyzes network packets; it does not manage firewall configuration."],1],["x1",0,1,"Hosts in a Layer 2 zone on the firewall must reach servers behind a Layer 3 interface. What lets the firewall route traffic for that zone's VLAN?",["A VLAN interface on the VLAN object, added to a router","A second virtual wire between the Layer 2 ports","A tap interface on the same VLAN","A loopback interface in each Layer 2 zone"],0,"A VLAN interface is a Layer 3 interface for a VLAN object, so it can route Layer 2 zone traffic to other networks. A virtual wire only bridges two ports transparently and cannot route.","Domain 1: Layer 2 and VLAN interfaces",[null,"A virtual wire bridges two ports transparently and cannot route between networks.","A tap interface only receives a copy of traffic and never forwards it.","Loopback interfaces host services on logical IPs; they are not a gateway for a VLAN."],2],["x2",0,1,"An engineer wants the GlobalProtect portal on an IP address that stays up even if one physical interface fails, reachable through several interfaces. Which interface type fits?",["A tap interface","A loopback interface","A virtual wire subinterface","An HA3 interface"],1,"A loopback interface is a logical Layer 3 interface not tied to one physical port, so services on it stay reachable through any path. A tap interface only listens passively and cannot host services.","Domain 1: Loopback interfaces",["A tap interface only listens to mirrored traffic and cannot host a portal.",null,"Virtual wire interfaces have no IP addresses, so they cannot host a service.","HA3 is a packet-forwarding link between active/active peers, not a service interface."],2],["x3",0,1,"An admin cannot select a virtual wire interface when editing a zone. What is the most likely reason?",["The zone is not enabled for User-ID","The virtual wire interface lacks an IP address and netmask","The zone type does not match the interface type","Zone protection is attached to the zone"],2,"A zone's type must match its interfaces, so virtual wire interfaces go only in virtual wire zones. Virtual wire interfaces never have IP addresses, so that is not the cause.","Domain 1: Security zones",["User-ID enablement changes nothing about which interface types a zone can hold.","Virtual wire interfaces never have IP addresses, so a missing IP is normal and not the cause.",null,"Zone protection profiles work on any zone type and do not limit interface selection."],1],["x4",0,1,"Someone on the internet is sweeping many TCP ports on hosts in a DMZ zone. Which part of a zone protection profile detects and can block this scanning?",["Flood protection","Packet-based attack protection","Protocol protection","Reconnaissance protection"],3,"Reconnaissance protection detects TCP and UDP port scans and host sweeps and can alert or block. Flood protection limits packet rates such as SYN floods and does not look for scan patterns.","Domain 1: Zone protection profiles",["Flood protection limits packet rates such as SYN floods; it does not look for scan patterns.","Packet-based protection drops malformed or spoofed packets, not port scans.","Protocol protection filters non-IP protocols by Ethertype, not port scanning.",null],1],["x5",0,1,"Packets arrive on the internet zone with source addresses from the company's own internal subnets. Which zone protection setting drops these?",["Spoofed IP address check (packet-based)","SYN cookies under flood protection, with a low activate rate","Host sweep under reconnaissance protection","ICMP flood thresholds"],0,"The spoofed IP address check drops packets whose source would not be routed back out the ingress interface. SYN cookies handle SYN floods and do not check source address validity.","Domain 1: Packet-based attack protection",[null,"SYN cookies defend against SYN floods and do not check whether a source address is valid.","Host sweep detection looks for scans across many hosts, not spoofed internal sources.","ICMP flood thresholds limit ICMP rates and do not look at the source address."],2],["x6",0,1,"One critical web server needs its own per-destination connection limits, separate from the thresholds that protect the whole DMZ zone. What should be configured?",["A tighter zone protection profile applied to the whole DMZ zone","A DoS protection policy with a classified DoS protection profile","An interface management profile on the DMZ interface","A vulnerability protection profile on the allow rule"],1,"A DoS protection policy with a classified profile can apply thresholds to specific destinations such as one server. Zone protection applies to the entire ingress zone, so it cannot give one server its own limits.","Domain 1: DoS protection",["Zone protection applies to all traffic entering the zone, so it cannot give one server its own limits.",null,"An interface management profile controls access to firewall services like ping and SSH, not DoS thresholds.","Vulnerability protection matches exploit signatures; it does not limit connection rates."],3],["x7",0,1,"A branch firewall runs OSPF in area 1 and only needs a default route toward the core, not external routes from other domains. How should area 1 be configured?",["As a normal area with a lower cost","As the backbone area","As a stub area","With BGP instead of OSPF"],2,"A stub area blocks external LSAs and uses a default route, which suits a branch with one way out. Making it the backbone would bring in all routes, the opposite of the goal.","Domain 1: OSPF areas",["A normal area still receives external routes, which is what the branch wants to avoid.","The backbone area carries all inter-area routes, the opposite of the goal.",null,"Switching to BGP is a redesign and does not answer how to limit OSPF routes in the area."],2],["x8",0,1,"On a legacy virtual router, static routes to lab networks must be advertised to OSPF neighbors. What is required?",["A policy-based forwarding rule matching the lab subnets as destinations","A static route with a lower metric","An ECMP group containing the static routes","A redistribution profile used in the OSPF export rules"],3,"A redistribution profile selects routes such as static routes, and the OSPF export rules use it to advertise them. PBF only steers traffic and never advertises routes to neighbors.","Domain 1: Route redistribution",["PBF steers matching traffic but never advertises routes to OSPF neighbors.","A lower metric changes preference locally but does not advertise the route into OSPF.","ECMP spreads traffic over equal paths; it does not advertise routes.",null],2],["x9",0,1,"Hundreds of internal users must reach the internet sharing a single public IP address. Which source NAT type fits?",["DIPP","Dynamic IP","Static IP","Destination NAT"],0,"DIPP translates many private addresses to one public IP by changing source ports. Dynamic IP maps each host to its own address from a pool, so it needs many public addresses.","Domain 1: Source NAT",[null,"Dynamic IP gives each host its own address from a pool, so it needs many public IPs.","Static IP NAT maps one private address to one public address, not many to one.","Destination NAT changes the destination address of inbound traffic, not outbound sources."],1],["x10",0,1,"A mail server needs the same public IP for its outbound mail and for inbound connections from the internet, using as few NAT rules as possible. What fits?",["A DIPP rule translating the mail server to the interface address","Static IP source NAT with bi-directional enabled","Destination NAT with port translation","A U-turn NAT rule"],1,"Static IP NAT with bi-directional enabled makes a 1:1 mapping that works for outbound and inbound connections from one rule. DIPP shares addresses and cannot accept inbound connections.","Domain 1: Static NAT",["DIPP shares addresses using ports and does not accept inbound connections to the server.",null,"Destination NAT alone handles inbound traffic but not outbound mail using the same IP.","U-turn NAT is for internal clients reaching an internal server through its public IP."],2],["x11",0,1,"Internet users connect to the public IP on TCP 8443, and the traffic must reach an internal server listening on TCP 443. What should be configured?",["Source NAT with DIPP on the server-facing interface","A service route for TCP 443","Destination NAT with a translated port of 443","A virtual wire with port mapping"],2,"Destination NAT can change both the destination address and port, so 8443 on the public IP maps to 443 on the server. A service route only chooses which interface the firewall's own services use.","Domain 1: Destination NAT",["Source NAT changes the source of the traffic, not the destination port to the server.","A service route picks the interface for the firewall's own services, not NAT for inbound traffic.",null,"Virtual wires have no IP addresses and do not perform port mapping."],1],["x12",0,1,"Traffic to a partner over an IPsec tunnel is being source-NATed by the general outbound DIPP rule, which breaks the tunnel's proxy IDs. How should this be fixed?",["Delete the DIPP rule and give all users static NAT addresses instead","Change the tunnel to use GRE","Add a policy-based forwarding rule for the partner","Add a no-NAT rule for the partner traffic above the DIPP rule"],3,"NAT rules are evaluated top down and the first match wins, so a no-NAT rule placed above the general rule keeps partner traffic untranslated. Deleting the DIPP rule would break internet access for everyone else.","Domain 1: NAT rule order",["Removing the DIPP rule breaks internet access for everyone to fix one partner.","GRE does not solve the NAT problem, and the peer expects IPsec.","PBF changes the egress path but does not stop the NAT rule from translating the traffic.",null],3],["x13",0,1,"Users in 10.1.1.0/24 in the Trust zone are source-NATed with DIPP to a public IP on the Untrust interface. Which source address should the outbound security rule use?",["10.1.1.0/24, the pre-NAT source","The public IP, the post-NAT source","Any, because NAT rules override security rules","The Untrust interface's IP address"],0,"Security rules match the original, pre-NAT source address, so the rule uses 10.1.1.0/24. The post-NAT public IP is what the internet sees, but the rule is evaluated against the original packet.","Domain 1: NAT and security policy",[null,"The post-NAT address is what the internet sees, but security rules match the original packet's source.","NAT rules never override security rules; both must allow the traffic.","The interface IP is only the translated address, not the source the rule is evaluated against."],2],["x14",0,1,"In an active/passive pair, if the single HA1 link fails, both firewalls could become active. What should be configured to reduce this risk?",["An HA3 link","An HA1 backup link","A second HA2 link in the same port","Preemption on the passive firewall"],1,"An HA1 backup link keeps control traffic and heartbeats flowing if the main HA1 link fails, avoiding split brain. HA3 exists only in active/active and forwards packets; it does not carry heartbeats.","Domain 1: HA links",["HA3 exists only in active/active and forwards packets; it carries no heartbeats.",null,"A second HA2 link protects session sync, not the control traffic and heartbeats on HA1.","Preemption decides which firewall becomes active after recovery; it does not prevent split brain."],2],["x15",0,1,"An active firewall has two uplinks in an HA link monitoring group. It should fail over only when both uplinks are down, not when just one fails. How should the group be set?",["Failure condition Any","Preemption hold time of zero","Failure condition All","Path monitoring with one destination"],2,"With failure condition All, the group fails only when every link in it is down. Any would fail over when just one uplink drops, which is exactly what they want to avoid.","Domain 1: HA link monitoring",["Any would fail over as soon as one uplink drops, which the team does not want.","Preemption hold time controls when a firewall takes back the active role, not link failure logic.",null,"Path monitoring checks remote IPs, not whether both local uplinks are down."],3],["x16",0,1,"To speed up failover, the team wants the passive firewall's data interfaces to show link up so neighboring switches keep them ready, without passing traffic. Which setting fits?",["Passive link state Shutdown","Floating IP address","HA2 keep-alive","Passive link state Auto"],3,"Passive link state Auto keeps the passive firewall's links physically up, which cuts failover time, while it still does not forward traffic. Shutdown is the default and keeps links down, which slows convergence.","Domain 1: HA passive link state",["Shutdown keeps the passive firewall's links down, which slows failover.","A floating IP is used in active/active designs; it does not keep passive links up.","HA2 keep-alive monitors the session sync link, not the data interfaces' link state.",null],3],["x17",0,1,"A branch has a primary and a backup IPsec tunnel to HQ. When the primary tunnel stops passing traffic, routing should move to the backup. What should be configured on the primary tunnel?",["A longer IKE lifetime and a larger DH group on the primary tunnel","Tunnel monitoring with a fail over monitor profile","A new IPsec crypto profile","Proxy IDs set to 0.0.0.0/0"],1,"Tunnel monitoring pings a destination across the tunnel and, with a fail over profile, takes the tunnel down so routes move to the backup. A longer IKE lifetime only changes when keys are renegotiated.","Domain 1: Tunnel monitoring",["A longer IKE lifetime and bigger DH group change keying, not failure detection.",null,"A new crypto profile changes phase 2 settings but does not detect a dead tunnel.","Wide proxy IDs change the traffic selectors, not how failure is detected."],2],["x18",0,1,"A branch gets its public IP from the ISP by DHCP, so it changes. How should the HQ firewall's IKE gateway for this branch be configured?",["Peer address type Dynamic, identifying the branch by peer ID","Peer address type IP, set to whatever address the branch has today","Peer address type FQDN pointing at the HQ firewall","No IKE gateway, with the branch using GRE instead"],0,"With a dynamic peer, HQ accepts the branch from any address and uses the peer ID to match it, while the branch starts the tunnel. A static IP peer would break the next time the branch's address changes.","Domain 1: IKE gateways",[null,"A fixed IP peer breaks as soon as the ISP gives the branch a new address.","An FQDN pointing at HQ describes the wrong end; HQ needs to identify the branch.","Dropping IKE removes encryption, and the requirement is an IPsec tunnel."],3],["x19",0,1,"Inside the office, laptops should still send HIP data and user information to the firewall for policy, but traffic should not be tunneled. What should be deployed?",["An external gateway with split tunneling for office subnets","A second portal for internal users","An internal gateway with internal host detection","The on-demand connect method"],2,"An internal gateway, found through internal host detection, gives User-ID and HIP data for on-site users without tunneling. An external gateway is for remote users and would tunnel traffic.","Domain 1: GlobalProtect gateways",["An external gateway would tunnel traffic, which the office does not want.","A second portal only delivers configuration; it does not provide User-ID and HIP enforcement.",null,"On-demand is a connect method; it does not provide an internal non-tunnel gateway."],3],["x20",0,1,"Contractors should connect to GlobalProtect only when they choose to, by clicking Connect. Which connect method fits?",["User-logon (always on)","Pre-logon (always on)","Pre-logon then on-demand","On-demand"],3,"On-demand connects only when the user starts the connection manually. User-logon connects automatically every time the user signs in, which is not what contractors want.","Domain 1: GlobalProtect connect methods",["User-logon connects automatically at every sign-in, not only when the contractor chooses.","Pre-logon connects before sign-in, automatically, which is the opposite of optional.","Pre-logon then on-demand still tunnels automatically before the user signs in.",null],1],["x21",0,1,"Users authenticate with MFA at the GlobalProtect portal and are prompted again at the gateway right after. How can the second prompt be avoided while keeping MFA?",["Authentication override cookies","Remove authentication from the portal","Change the connect method to on-demand","Use an SSL tunnel instead of IPsec"],0,"Authentication override lets the portal issue a cookie that the gateway accepts, so users authenticate once. Removing portal authentication would weaken security and still leave the gateway prompt.","Domain 1: GlobalProtect authentication",[null,"Removing portal authentication weakens security and the gateway would still prompt.","Connect method changes when the app connects, not how many times users authenticate.","The tunnel type has no effect on authentication prompts."],3],["x22",0,1,"Branch clients use the firewall as their DNS server. Queries for corp.example must go to internal DNS servers and all others to public resolvers. What should be configured?",["A DHCP relay agent","A DNS proxy object with a domain-specific rule","A destination NAT rule sending all UDP 53 traffic to the internal servers","A service route for DNS"],1,"A DNS proxy can forward queries for chosen domains to specific servers and send everything else to default servers. A service route only changes which interface the firewall's own DNS queries use.","Domain 1: DNS proxy",["A DHCP relay forwards DHCP requests, not DNS queries.",null,"Destination NAT sends all DNS to one place and cannot split by domain.","A service route only affects the firewall's own DNS queries, not client queries."],2],["x23",0,1,"The ISP hands out the firewall's outside address by DHCP. Which setting on the interface's DHCP client installs a default route toward the ISP's gateway?",["Enable the DHCP server on the interface","Add a management profile allowing ping","Create a default route from the server-provided gateway","Configure a service route for DHCP"],2,"The DHCP client option to create a default route from the server-provided gateway installs the route automatically. A DHCP server hands out addresses to clients; it does not learn anything from the ISP.","Domain 1: DHCP client interfaces",["The DHCP server hands out addresses to clients; it does not learn a route from the ISP.","A management profile controls which services respond on the interface, not routing.",null,"Service routes choose source interfaces for firewall services and do not install default routes."],1],["x24",0,2,"An authentication profile uses LDAP for admin logins, but only members of the FW-Admins group should be able to log in through it. Which setting restricts this?",["The base DN in the LDAP server profile, narrowed to the admins OU","The allow list in the authentication profile","An authentication sequence","A certificate profile"],1,"The allow list in the authentication profile limits which users or groups can authenticate with it. The base DN only sets where in the directory to search and does not limit who may log in.","Domain 2: Authentication profiles",["Narrowing the base DN changes where searches start but is not the intended access control and is easy to get wrong.",null,"An authentication sequence tries several profiles in order; it does not limit which users succeed.","A certificate profile validates certificates, not LDAP group membership."],2],["x25",0,2,"Admins should sign in to the firewall web interface through the company's identity provider using single sign-on. What should be configured?",["A SAML IdP server profile used in an authentication profile","A RADIUS server profile with a shared secret, used in an authentication sequence","A Kerberos server profile only","A local user database"],0,"SAML with an IdP server profile gives browser-based single sign-on to the web interface. RADIUS checks credentials against a server but is not browser SSO through an identity provider.","Domain 2: SAML authentication",[null,"RADIUS checks credentials but does not give browser single sign-on through the IdP.","Kerberos alone does not provide SSO through the company's cloud identity provider.","Local accounts bypass the identity provider entirely."],1],["x26",0,2,"Domain-joined users whose User-ID mapping is missing hit the Authentication Portal. They should be authenticated transparently by their browsers without typing credentials. What fits?",["LDAP authentication with the portal's login form and a remembered username","Local database accounts","Kerberos single sign-on with a keytab in the authentication profile","RADIUS with a shared secret"],2,"Kerberos SSO lets browsers of domain-joined users authenticate to the Authentication Portal without prompts, using the keytab. LDAP still shows a login form, so users would have to type credentials.","Domain 2: Authentication Portal",["LDAP still shows a login form, so users must type credentials.","Local database accounts need a login prompt and separate passwords.",null,"RADIUS needs the user to enter credentials in the portal."],3],["x27",0,2,"The Authentication Portal should use session cookies so users are not prompted repeatedly and should redirect browsers to a Layer 3 interface. Which portal mode fits?",["Transparent mode","Tap mode","Virtual wire mode","Redirect mode"],3,"Redirect mode sends users to a Layer 3 interface hosting the portal and supports session cookies. Transparent mode impersonates the original destination and does not provide those cookies.","Domain 2: Authentication Portal modes",["Transparent mode impersonates the original destination and does not use session cookies.","Tap mode is an interface deployment type, not an Authentication Portal mode.","Virtual wire is an interface type, not a portal mode.",null],3],["x28",0,2,"Authentication policy must trigger a push from the company's third-party MFA vendor before users reach a sensitive app. What does the firewall need to talk to that vendor?",["An MFA server profile for the vendor","A SAML certificate profile","An HTTP server profile","A syslog server profile"],0,"An MFA server profile connects the firewall to supported MFA vendors so the authentication profile can add that factor. An HTTP server profile sends logs to web services and is not used for authentication.","Domain 2: Multi-factor authentication",[null,"Certificate profiles validate certificates and do not connect to an MFA vendor.","HTTP server profiles send logs to web services and play no part in authentication.","Syslog profiles send logs, not authentication requests."],2],["x29",0,2,"The NOC wants HA failover events and other System log entries sent to syslog. Where is this configured?",["A log forwarding profile on security rules","Device > Log Settings for System logs","The syslog server profile only","A zone protection profile"],1,"System logs are forwarded from Device > Log Settings, not from security rules. Log forwarding profiles on rules handle traffic and threat logs from sessions, not system events.","Domain 2: Log settings",["Log forwarding profiles on rules handle session logs like traffic and threat, not system events.",null,"A server profile only defines the destination; something must select which logs to send.","Zone protection profiles defend zones and have nothing to do with log forwarding."],2],["x30",0,2,"An admin wants every newly created security rule to use a standard log forwarding profile automatically. What should they do?",["Attach the profile to each zone","Set it in Device > Log Settings","Name the log forwarding profile default","Reference it in an authentication sequence for new rules"],2,"A log forwarding profile named default is applied to new security rules automatically. Device > Log Settings controls system, config and similar logs, not the profile on new rules.","Domain 2: Log forwarding profiles",["Zones do not hold log forwarding profiles, and security rules are where the profile is set.","Device > Log Settings controls system and config logs, not the default profile on new rules.",null,"Authentication sequences are for user login, not logging."],2],["x31",0,2,"A network monitoring system must receive alerts from the firewall as traps. What should be created and referenced in log forwarding?",["An email server profile","A syslog server profile","An HTTP server profile","An SNMP trap server profile"],3,"An SNMP trap server profile tells the firewall where and how to send traps. A syslog profile sends syslog messages, which an NMS expecting traps would not receive.","Domain 2: SNMP",["An email profile sends email, which an NMS expecting traps would not receive.","Syslog messages are a different protocol from SNMP traps.","HTTP profiles send web requests, not SNMP traps.",null],1],["x32",0,2,"The team wants the firewall to receive new WildFire malware signatures as soon as Palo Alto Networks publishes them. How should the WildFire update schedule be set?",["Real-time","Daily with a 24-hour threshold","Weekly","Download only, with manual install"],0,"Real-time WildFire updates pull new signatures as they become available, giving the fastest protection. A daily schedule with a threshold deliberately delays them.","Domain 2: Dynamic updates",[null,"A daily schedule with a threshold delays new signatures, the opposite of the goal.","Weekly updates leave long gaps against new malware.","Manual install delays protection until someone acts."],2],["x33",0,2,"With SSL forward proxy on, the firewall should refuse sessions to sites whose certificates have expired. Where is this set?",["In the SSL/TLS service profile","In the decryption profile's server certificate checks","In the certificate profile used by the GlobalProtect portal and gateway","In the URL filtering profile"],1,"The decryption profile has server certificate verification options such as blocking sessions with expired certificates. An SSL/TLS service profile controls the firewall's own services like the portal, not decrypted traffic.","Domain 2: Decryption profiles",["SSL/TLS service profiles govern the firewall's own services, not decrypted sessions.",null,"The GlobalProtect certificate profile validates client certificates, not website certificates.","URL filtering controls categories, not certificate validity."],2],["x34",0,2,"Legal says employees' banking and health sites must not be decrypted for privacy, while other web traffic is. What is the recommended approach?",["A no-decrypt rule for those URL categories","Turn off decryption for the whole user zone","Remove the forward trust certificate from clients","A security rule that denies those categories"],0,"A no-decrypt decryption rule using URL categories keeps those sites private while other traffic is still decrypted. Turning off decryption for the whole zone loses inspection of everything else.","Domain 2: Decryption exclusions",[null,"Turning off decryption for the whole zone loses inspection of all other traffic.","Removing the trust certificate causes warnings everywhere and does not target those categories.","Denying the categories blocks the sites instead of letting users reach them privately."],2],["x35",0,2,"After SSL forward proxy was enabled, users see certificate warnings on every HTTPS site. What is the most likely fix?",["Import each website's private key","Switch to SSL inbound inspection","Deploy the forward trust CA certificate to clients' trusted root store","Use the forward untrust certificate for all decrypted sites so trust is consistent"],2,"Clients must trust the CA that signs the firewall's impersonated certificates, so the forward trust CA must be deployed to them. Using forward untrust for everything would make every site show a warning on purpose.","Domain 2: SSL forward proxy certificates",["Forward proxy does not use websites' private keys, and nobody could obtain them.","Inbound inspection is for servers the company owns, not users' outbound browsing.",null,"Forward untrust is meant to warn users on bad certificates, so using it everywhere forces warnings on every site."],2],["x36",0,2,"The company wants the forward trust certificate issued by its enterprise PKI so managed devices trust it already. What is the recommended way?",["Import the enterprise root CA's certificate and private key into the firewall as forward trust","Use a self-signed forward trust certificate","Use the web server's certificate","Generate a CSR on the firewall and have the enterprise CA sign it as a subordinate CA"],3,"A CSR signed as a subordinate CA gives the firewall a signing certificate that chains to the trusted enterprise root. Importing the root CA's private key would expose the most valuable key in the PKI.","Domain 2: Certificate management",["Putting the root CA's private key on a firewall exposes the most sensitive key in the PKI.","A self-signed certificate is not trusted by managed devices without extra deployment.","A web server's certificate is not a CA certificate and cannot sign other certificates.",null],3],["x37",0,2,"A certificate profile checks client certificates with OCSP. If the OCSP responder does not answer in time, the session must be blocked. Which option should be enabled?",["Block session if certificate status is unknown, with CRL as a fallback check","Block session if certificate status cannot be retrieved within timeout","Use CRL only","Block sessions with untrusted issuers"],1,"The timeout option blocks the session when no status comes back in time, which is the case described. 'Status is unknown' applies when the responder answers but does not know the certificate.","Domain 2: Certificate profiles and OCSP",["Unknown status is when the responder replies but does not know the certificate, not a timeout.",null,"Using CRL only changes the method; it does not block when the check times out.","Untrusted issuer checks the chain, not the revocation response time."],3],["x38",0,2,"Security rules must reference Active Directory groups. What must be configured so the firewall knows group membership?",["Server monitoring of logon events on the domain controllers","An authentication sequence","Group mapping with an LDAP server profile","A certificate profile"],2,"Group mapping reads group membership from LDAP so rules can use groups. Server monitoring maps IP addresses to users but does not tell the firewall which groups users belong to.","Domain 2: Group mapping",["Server monitoring maps IPs to users but does not supply group membership.","Authentication sequences try login sources in order; they do not read groups.",null,"Certificate profiles validate certificates and do not supply groups."],1],["x39",0,2,"A network access control system already knows which user is on each IP and wants to send those mappings to the firewall. Which User-ID method fits?",["Server monitoring","Client probing","Authentication Portal","The User-ID XML API"],3,"The User-ID XML API lets external systems push user mappings to the firewall. Server monitoring reads domain controller logs and cannot receive mappings from a NAC system.","Domain 2: User-ID sources",["Server monitoring reads domain controller logs and cannot take mappings from a NAC.","Client probing queries Windows clients, not a NAC system.","Authentication Portal prompts users directly and ignores the NAC's knowledge.",null],1],["x40",0,2,"Remote users connect with GlobalProtect. How does the firewall learn their user-to-IP mappings?",["GlobalProtect supplies them on connect","The user must pass the Authentication Portal","Server monitoring of VPN logs is required","A syslog listener must parse the VPN logs"],0,"GlobalProtect sends the user identity with each connection, so the firewall maps the user to the assigned IP. Authentication Portal is a fallback for unknown users, not needed for GlobalProtect users.","Domain 2: User-ID with GlobalProtect",[null,"Authentication Portal is a fallback for unknown users, not needed for GlobalProtect users.","Server monitoring reads domain controllers, and GlobalProtect users need no extra source.","A syslog listener is for third-party systems, not GlobalProtect connections."],1],["x41",0,2,"User-ID is creating mappings for a guest Wi-Fi subnet, which is not wanted. What should be adjusted?",["The group mapping search filter and the included groups list","The include and exclude networks list","The authentication sequence","The decryption policy"],1,"The include/exclude networks list controls which subnets User-ID maps. The group mapping filter controls which groups are read, not which IP ranges get user mappings.","Domain 2: User-ID settings",["The group mapping filter controls which groups are read, not which subnets get mappings.",null,"Authentication sequences affect login, not which IP ranges User-ID maps.","Decryption policy has no effect on User-ID mappings."],2],["x42",0,2,"Twenty branch firewalls all need the same user mappings, but only one hub collects them from User-ID sources. What should be used?",["A Windows User-ID agent installed for every branch firewall","Server monitoring from each branch","User-ID redistribution from the hub to the branches","A template variable for each user"],2,"Redistribution shares mappings collected on one firewall or Panorama with others, avoiding duplicate collection. Running agents at every branch repeats collection work and adds load on the domain controllers.","Domain 2: User-ID redistribution",["Agents for every branch repeat collection work and add load on the domain controllers.","Server monitoring from each branch also repeats collection against the same sources.",null,"Template variables hold device settings, not user mappings."],3],["x43",0,2,"An admin made several uncommitted changes and wants to throw them all away, returning to the running configuration. What should they do?",["Save a named snapshot","Commit the changes","Export the candidate configuration","Revert changes to the running configuration"],3,"Reverting discards candidate changes and returns to the running configuration. Saving a snapshot keeps the candidate config instead of discarding it.","Domain 2: Candidate and running configuration",["A snapshot saves the candidate configuration instead of discarding it.","Committing would make the unwanted changes live.","Exporting the candidate saves a copy but leaves the changes in place.",null],1],["x44",0,2,"On a multi-vsys firewall, a customer's admin must manage only vsys2. Which administrator type should be used?",["Virtual system administrator scoped to vsys2","Superuser","Device administrator (read-only)","Panorama administrator"],0,"A virtual system administrator role limits the admin to the chosen vsys. Superuser gives full access to the whole firewall, including other customers' vsys.","Domain 2: Admin roles and vsys",[null,"Superuser gives full access to the whole firewall, including other customers' vsys.","Device administrator read-only applies to the whole device and cannot make changes.","Panorama administrators manage Panorama, not a single vsys on the firewall."],1],["x45",0,2,"One customer's vsys must not consume all firewall sessions and starve the others. What should be configured?",["A zone protection profile on each zone","Resource limits such as maximum sessions on the vsys","A DoS protection policy in vsys1","A shared gateway"],1,"Vsys resource limits cap sessions, rules and other resources for each vsys. Zone protection handles floods and scans but does not divide firewall capacity between vsys.","Domain 2: Virtual systems",["Zone protection handles floods and scans; it does not split firewall capacity between vsys.",null,"A DoS policy in vsys1 protects vsys1's destinations but does not cap another vsys's sessions.","A shared gateway shares an interface; it does not limit resources."],2],["x46",0,2,"A branch wants web proxy protection but cannot change any browser or PAC settings on client devices. Which web proxy mode fits?",["Explicit proxy","SOCKS proxy","Transparent proxy","Reverse proxy"],2,"A transparent proxy intercepts web traffic without client configuration. An explicit proxy needs browsers or a PAC file pointed at it, which is not allowed here.","Domain 2: Web proxy",["An explicit proxy needs browsers or a PAC file set to use it, which is not allowed here.","SOCKS also needs client configuration and is not the PAN-OS web proxy mode described.",null,"A reverse proxy fronts servers for inbound users, not outbound browsing."],1],["x47",0,3,"In Panorama, a device group hierarchy has Branches under Region-East. An address object must be usable by every firewall in both groups. Where should it be defined?",["In each branch firewall locally","In a template","In the Branches device group only, then clone it upward","In Region-East, the parent device group"],3,"Objects in a parent device group are inherited by its children, so defining it in Region-East covers both groups. Templates hold network and device settings, not policy objects like addresses.","Domain 3: Device group hierarchy",["Local objects on each firewall cannot be managed centrally and must be kept in sync by hand.","Templates hold network and device settings, not policy objects.","Branches alone would not cover firewalls directly in Region-East.",null],2],["x48",0,3,"Panorama must add a deny-all rule with logging that is evaluated only after local firewall rules. Where should it go?",["Post-rules in the device group","Pre-rules in the device group","A template","The firewall's default rules"],0,"Post-rules are evaluated after local rules, making them the place for a final deny with logging. Pre-rules run before local rules, so a deny there would block traffic local rules should allow.","Domain 3: Pre-rules and post-rules",[null,"Pre-rules run before local rules, so a deny there would override the local allow rules.","Templates hold device and network settings, not security rules.","The predefined default rules only allow intrazone and deny interzone traffic; overriding them changes settings like logging but does not create a catch-all deny."],1],["x49",0,3,"A template stack contains Template-Branch and Template-Global, and both set a different NTP server. Which value do the firewalls receive?",["Template-Global always wins because it is shared","The value from whichever template was edited and committed most recently","The value from the template higher in the stack list","Neither, and the push fails"],2,"In a template stack, templates higher in the list take priority when settings overlap. Edit time does not decide precedence, so the most recent edit is not what wins.","Domain 3: Template stacks",["Shared status does not decide precedence; stack order does.","Edit time does not affect which template wins.",null,"Overlapping settings are resolved by stack order, so the push does not fail."],3],["x50",0,3,"After a commit to Panorama, the admin wants to push the device group change only to one firewall during a maintenance window. What should they do?",["Commit locally on every firewall","Edit the push scope to select only that firewall","Move the firewall to a new template stack and device group for the push","Export the configuration to the firewall"],1,"The push scope can be edited to include only the chosen firewall. Committing locally on firewalls does not bring Panorama's pending changes to them.","Domain 3: Panorama push",["Local commits do not bring Panorama's pending changes to firewalls.",null,"Moving the firewall changes its configuration source, which is a bigger change than needed.","Exporting and importing config by hand bypasses Panorama's push workflow."],2],["x51",0,3,"A Panorama appliance should manage firewalls while logs go to separate dedicated Log Collectors, not to Panorama itself. Which mode fits?",["Log Collector mode","Panorama mode with local log storage","Management Only mode","Legacy mode on a VM"],2,"Management Only mode manages firewalls and relies on dedicated Log Collectors for logs. Log Collector mode is for appliances that only collect logs and cannot manage firewalls.","Domain 3: Panorama modes",["Log Collector mode only collects logs and cannot manage firewalls.","Panorama mode stores logs locally, which the design wants to avoid.",null,"Legacy mode is an older VM mode and is not the dedicated management design described."],1],["x52",0,3,"A script needs the firewall's system information, like the output of the CLI command show system info, from the XML API. Which request type fits?",["type=config","type=keygen","type=commit","type=op"],3,"type=op runs operational commands such as show system info. type=config reads or changes configuration, not operational state.","Domain 3: XML API",["type=config reads or changes configuration, not operational state.","type=keygen returns an API key and runs no commands.","type=commit activates configuration changes and returns no system info.",null],1],["x53",0,3,"A script uses the XML API to add one new member to an address group without removing the existing members. Which config action fits?",["action=set","action=edit","action=delete","action=rename"],0,"set adds or merges elements at the xpath without removing existing ones. edit replaces the element at the xpath with what is sent, which would drop existing members not included.","Domain 3: XML API",[null,"edit replaces the element with what is sent, dropping members not included.","delete removes configuration, the opposite of adding a member.","rename changes an object's name and does not add members."],3],["x54",0,3,"A script successfully changed security rules through the XML API, but the firewall is still enforcing the old rules. What was missed?",["A new API key","A commit request","A content update","A reboot of the firewall"],1,"API changes go into the candidate configuration and only take effect after a commit. A new API key only affects authentication, not whether changes are active.","Domain 3: API and commits",["The existing key worked, since the changes were accepted.",null,"Content updates add signatures and do not activate configuration changes.","Rebooting does not commit the candidate configuration."],2],["x55",0,3,"The SOC publishes a list of malicious domains and wants DNS queries for them sinkholed or blocked by the firewall. How should the domain list be used?",["As an IP address EDL in a security rule","As a URL EDL in a decryption rule","As FQDN address objects in a dynamic address group used by a deny rule","As a domain EDL in an anti-spyware profile's DNS policies"],3,"A domain EDL can be added to an anti-spyware profile's DNS policies to sinkhole or block queries. An IP EDL contains IP addresses, so it cannot match domain names in DNS queries.","Domain 3: External dynamic lists",["IP EDLs hold addresses, so they cannot match domain names in DNS queries.","Decryption rules decide what to decrypt, not what DNS queries to block.","Address objects need manual edits and are not designed for large, frequently changing domain feeds.",null],3],["x56",0,3,"An EDL's source file changes every hour. How does the firewall pick up the changes?",["Commit after every update","Set the EDL's check for updates interval to hourly","Reboot the firewall daily","Push from Panorama each hour"],1,"The EDL object's update interval makes the firewall refresh the list itself, with no commit needed. Committing after every change is what EDLs are meant to avoid.","Domain 3: External dynamic lists",["EDLs refresh without a commit, so committing after each change is unnecessary work.",null,"Rebooting does not refresh an EDL and causes an outage.","Panorama pushes are for configuration changes, not EDL content refresh."],2],["x57",0,3,"The SOC wants users, not IP addresses, blocked automatically when threat logs show they downloaded malware. Which object pairs with auto-tagging to do this?",["An address group with static members","A service group","A dynamic user group matching the tag","A HIP profile"],2,"Dynamic user groups include users by tag, so auto-tagging a user adds them to a group that a blocking rule uses. A static address group needs manual changes and works on IPs, not users.","Domain 3: Dynamic user groups",["Static groups need manual changes and hold IPs, not users.","Service groups hold ports and protocols, not users.",null,"HIP profiles match endpoint posture, not tags from threat logs."],2],["x58",0,3,"An auto-tag quarantines hosts that contact a C2 server, but the SOC wants the tag removed automatically after a day unless renewed. What should they set?",["A timeout on the tag action in the log forwarding profile","A template variable","A weekly EDL refresh","A post-rule with a schedule"],0,"The auto-tag action can set a timeout so the tag and quarantine expire without manual cleanup. A scheduled post-rule changes when a rule applies, not how long a host stays tagged.","Domain 3: Auto-tagging",[null,"Template variables hold device settings and cannot expire tags.","An EDL refresh does not remove tags from hosts.","A scheduled rule changes when a rule applies, not how long a host stays tagged."],2],["x59",0,3,"A VM-Series firewall in AWS must bootstrap at first boot. Where is the bootstrap package normally stored for AWS?",["On a USB drive or ISO image attached to the instance at launch","In an S3 bucket read through the instance's IAM role","In the Panorama log collector","In a GitHub repository"],1,"On AWS, the bootstrap package sits in an S3 bucket, and an IAM role lets the instance read it. USB or ISO media suits some on-premises deployments, not an AWS instance.","Domain 3: VM-Series bootstrapping",["USB or ISO media suits some on-premises hypervisors; cloud instances use cloud storage.",null,"Log collectors store logs, not bootstrap packages.","Bootstrap packages come from cloud storage, not a Git repository."],2],["x60",0,3,"A VM-Series firewall must license itself during bootstrap. Where should the auth codes go in the bootstrap package?",["In init-cfg.txt as a comment","In the content folder","In the software folder","In the authcodes file in the license folder"],3,"The license folder holds an authcodes file that the firewall uses to register licenses at bootstrap. The content folder holds content update packages, not licenses.","Domain 3: VM-Series bootstrapping",["init-cfg.txt holds first-boot settings like Panorama IPs, not license auth codes in comments.","The content folder holds content updates, not licenses.","The software folder holds PAN-OS images, not auth codes.",null],2]]);
